A scroll's `@reachEnd` reaches its handler, wired to what each target has for
telling that the end of the content is in view.

None of them has an event saying so. android renders SnglScroll, Kotlin the
platform ships, whose effect watches the scroll state and the content's
measured height. html writes a `sngl-reach-end` element after the content,
which the page defines: an IntersectionObserver on it and a ResizeObserver on
the content dispatch `reachEnd`, an event html.element declares so a test's
`c.list.reachEnd()` names the event dispatched. gtk4 builds gtk4rt's Scroll, a
GtkScrolledWindow whose vertical adjustment says how far it has gone and how
tall the content is, and fyne fynelayout's ReachScroll, which looks again a
moment after each report since Fyne says nothing when content grows.

Each one fires as the end arrives, and content too short to scroll has its
end in view at once, so the rows here are tall enough that the end of the
first forty is out of view: the test's trigger is then the only thing that
can add a page. android's trigger is the scroll's own ScrollBy semantics
action, which SnglScroll reports as it would a finger's; a trigger with no
Compose action was a comment, which a page arriving on its own let pass.

bubbletea draws a scroll as its content, cut off by the terminal, and fires
nothing: no key reaches a layout, so a test's trigger runs the handler as the
interpreter does, through an invoker written for an event a test fires on a
layout.

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:test"
import seq "sngl:seq"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main ui.node {
    var shown = 40

    ui.scroll #list(style={flex=1}, @reachEnd {
        if shown < 60 {
            shown += 10
        }
    }) {
        ui.vbox {
            for var i = seq.range(0, shown) {
                ui.text(value="row {i}", style={height=48px})
            }
        }
    }
}

func testReachEndLoadsAPage(t test.Test, c main) {
    var before = c.shown
    c.list.reachEnd()
    t.assert(c.shown > before)
}

ui.window {
    main
}
